{{!-- WHAT A SEASON IS CALLED, WHEREVER IT IS PRINTED.

      One heading, two hosts: the block on a single-track spine, and the row head on the aggregate
      board. They lay their surroundings out differently but they name a season the same way, and
      when this was written out twice the two could drift -- which on a chronicle means the same
      season reading as two different ones depending on which view you opened.

      ⚠ THE COLOUR IS THE HOST'S, NOT THIS HEADING'S. Each host wears the period's `seasonClass`,
      which paints it in the timeline's own season colour (never the four `--stonetop-season-*-ink`
      tokens, which belong to the steading header's clock; a test counts every read of them). The
      name and the glyph here still say WHICH season, so the colour is never the only signal.

      The undated block has no season and no year, so it prints its own label and no chip.

      Context: one period view-model (timeline-view.js#periodVM). --}}
<h3 class="stonetop-timeline-period-title{{#if glyphClass}} stonetop-season-entry {{glyphClass}}{{/if}}">
	{{#if undated}}{{label}}{{else}}{{seasonLabel}}{{/if}}
</h3>
{{#unless undated}}
<span class="stonetop-year-chip">{{yearLabel}}</span>
{{/unless}}
